        <p class="fund-subtitle" data-longform="5" data-longform-label="Read the full brief">The 30-year Treasury closed <strong>5.18% on Aug 26, 2026</strong> — a 5 bp rally across the week as the buyback bid returned, 13 bp under the Aug 17 record (5.31) — leaving <strong>82 bp</strong> to a 6-handle that has not printed since May 2000. This page hunts every Polymarket book that <strong>pays off if the 30-year touches 6.00% before Dec 31, 2026</strong>, prices each one against a disclosed model, and maps what a 6% long bond does to the economy and the stock market. There is no direct 30-year market on Polymarket — the closest tradeable instrument is the <strong>10-year “how high” ladder</strong>, plus the Fed-hike complex, the SPX down-touch ladder, and the macro side bets. Odds refresh live from the Polymarket CLOB every 60 seconds; books re-walked <strong>Aug 27, 2026 13:49 UTC (Wednesday post-print morning walk)</strong>. <strong>Three things changed tonight, and two of them are corrections to this page.</strong> First, the yield pipeline is fixed: federalreserve.gov H.15 and Treasury.gov par yields now feed the run directly, so the curve is current for the first time since Jul 30 — and it shows this page has been quoting a stale record (the 2026 high as of Aug 10 was <strong>5.27% on Jul 31</strong> — since taken out by 5.31 on Aug 17 — not the 5.21% led with here for eleven days). Second, <strong>P(30Y touches 6%) is cut from 18% to 5.4%</strong>: re-derived on the new curve, two-thirds of the old number was drift extrapolated from a coefficient with a t-statistic of 0.84, and the old 18% never reproduced from its own stated inputs. Third, and most important, <strong>the “largest edge on leadsure.com” is withdrawn</strong> — an equal-weight mixture of the two models that actually fit this series prices the 5.0% rung at 35.0 against a 34.00 market and the 5.2% rung at 13.8 against 14.15. The ladder did double on the day (the 5.0% anchor went <strong>17.0 → 34.0</strong> and the position marks <strong>+$1,015</strong>), but the bid side of that book is <strong>1,047 shares and $178.67</strong> against an 8,880-share position: 88% of it has no bid at any price. Fictitious research page — not financial advice.</p>
