            <p>Read the rule before the model. The leg resolves YES only if CPI “increased by <em>greater than</em> the listed percent” on the official BLS release, “which reports inflation over 12 month periods to only one decimal point” — so a printed 4.5% resolves NO and <strong>the true barrier is 4.6%</strong>. Getting there is arithmetic, not forecasting: headline sits at <strong>3.5%</strong> with core at <strong>2.6% and falling</strong>, and five prints remain in the 2026 series, so the y/y has to accelerate <strong>+1.1 points</strong> and hold. The tab publishes no CPI model and is not pretending to; what it can quote is the deepest two-sided book on the page — <strong>20 × 1,712.21 / 21 × 764.24</strong>, with $959.94 of bids inside 19¢ — and a barrier that moved a tenth in our favour on a close reading.</p>
