            <p>Three ladders on this tab, three smiles, and one of them is upside down. The 10-year runs <strong>1.94 → 5.87 bp/day</strong> across strikes and the SPX runs <strong>24.6% → 33.3%</strong> — both rising, both what a tail-fearing market looks like. Gold, on the tab’s own published barrier distances of +22% and +46% and the same 143-day clock, runs <strong>42.1% → 36.2%: falling</strong>. The far strike is priced with <em>less</em> volatility than the near one, in the single market whose entire story is a monetary accident. Note what this is and is not: the tab just retracted a constant-σ result as an artefact, so the valid instrument here is the <em>slope inside one ladder</em>, not a level compared across ladders that monitor differently — gold settles on CME closes, SPX on 1-minute candles. The slope says the account bought the expensive rung at 46.2¢ on Monday.</p>
