        <p class="pm-footnote" data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read the full footnote"><strong>EXECUTION NOTE (Aug 11):</strong> #12 filled as written — 150.48 sh of the $419–426K bucket at 33.89¢ (ledger row 143); the card stays as the hold. Rungs 1–2 of the 10Y ladder were bought AGAINST this tab’s adds-off rule (rows 144/146, 500 sh through the fired kill-switch) — logged as divergences. Twelve slots, <strong>ten of them executable tickets</strong> — the largest actionable share this tab has ever carried, and a deliberate correction: the previous slate was twelve pieces of analysis and one instruction, which is not a trading page. What changed is where the tab looks. The 10-year ladder is 8.4% of the rates complex by book depth and 100% of the tab’s prior attention; the Fed complex alone shows <strong>372,401.1 shares and $153,211.0 of bids inside 40¢ on the Sept leg</strong>, against the ladder’s entire 19-level bid side of <strong>$178.67</strong> — a ratio of <strong>857×</strong>. Four of the twelve tickets cost nothing to place (#1, #4, #7, #9 are resting orders); five commit cash (#2 $350, #3 $300, #5 $250, #6 $200, #8 $200 = <strong>$1,300</strong>); one is portfolio hygiene with a hard basket cap (#10); two are dated watches with liquidity triggers (#11, #12). Every size on this page was walked against the book printed on its own card at <strong>22:29 UTC Aug 10, Monday post-close</strong>, asset_id and timestamp verified in-run. Nothing here contradicts the four findings the tab published tonight: the ladder still marks $3,582.74 and liquidates for $268.35, the 34.0 mid on rung 1 is still ours (last trade 27.0), P(30Y touches 6%) is still 5.4% martingale and 16.2% only if a t = 0.84 drift persists, and the ladder still prices a rising 1.94 → 5.87 bp/day smile that makes any constant-σ “near rungs are cheap” claim an artefact. The tickets are built on book depth, resolution text and the smile’s <em>slope</em> — none of them needs the retracted result to be true.</p>
