<section class="table-section" aria-label="Mispricing monitor the rates complex">
        <div class="table-header-row">
          <h2 class="section-title">Mispricing Monitor — The Rates Complex</h2>
          <span class="pm-sec-date">Ideas as of Aug 10, 2026 · re-checked Aug 27, 6:49 AM PT (Wednesday post-print morning walk)</span>
          <span class="price-note" data-longform="2" data-longform-label="Read the full note">Ten dislocations across the whole complex, not one ladder · observations only, no tickets — the tickets live above · tonight’s theme: <strong>the deep books are internally coherent and the thin ones are noise the page has been reading as signal</strong> · 4 shown, scroll → for more</span>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-cards">
          <div class="panel-card">
            <h3 class="panel-title"><span class="pm-badge pm-rich">1 · THREE LADDERS, THREE SMILES, ONE OF THEM INVERTED</span> 10-year 1.94 → 5.87 bp/day rising · SPX 24.6% → 33.3% rising · gold 42.1% → 36.2% <em>falling</em></h3>
            <div class="pm-idea-date">Noted Aug 10, 2026 <span class="pm-reviewed">· Reviewed Aug 27, 2026</span><a class="pm-card-link" href="https://polymarket.com/event/what-will-gold-gc-hit-by-end-of-december" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open this market on Polymarket">View on Polymarket ↗</a></div>
            <p>The tab learned tonight that inverting each rung to its own implied volatility is the honest way to read a ladder. Run it on all three ladders on the page, same 143-day clock, same driftless touch formula, barrier distances as already published here. The 10-year: <strong>1.94, 3.11, 3.38, 4.48, 4.97, 5.87 bp/day</strong> — monotone up. The SPX down-touches: <strong>24.6% at −17%, 27.9% at −23%, 33.3% at −31%</strong> — monotone up, a textbook equity put skew. Gold: <strong>42.1% at +22% and 36.2% at +46%</strong> — down. Two markets pay up for the tail and one marks it down, and the one that marks it down is the market whose only reason to exist is a monetary accident. Caveat stated plainly: gold monitors on CME settlements and SPX on 1-minute candles, so the cross-ladder <em>levels</em> are not comparable. The <em>slope</em> inside gold is, and it is the wrong sign.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-card">
            <h3 class="panel-title"><span class="pm-badge pm-watch">2 · THE KILL-SWITCH GOVERNS A $178.67 BOOK FROM A $153,211 ONE</span> Sept-hike 43.5 on 372,401.1 shares of bids inside 40¢ — 857× the ladder it controls</h3>
            <div class="pm-idea-date">Noted Aug 10, 2026 <span class="pm-reviewed">· Reviewed Aug 27, 2026</span><a class="pm-card-link" href="https://polymarket.com/event/fed-decision-in-september-762" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open this market on Polymarket">View on Polymarket ↗</a></div>
            <p>Put the two books side by side and the tab’s whole posture looks strange. The September hike leg quotes <strong>43 × 18,254 / 44 × 74,993</strong>, and inside 40¢ its bid stack holds <strong>372,401.1 shares and $153,211.0</strong>. The 5.0% rung it governs has a bid side of <strong>1,046.7 shares and $178.67</strong>. The ratio is <strong>857×</strong>. The page has spent eleven days extracting signal from the thin book and using the deep one only as an on/off switch — and the switch is still FIRED at <strong>1.5 points under its 45¢ trigger</strong>, re-arm requiring two full closes above it. The observation is not that the switch is wrong. It is that a market with $153,211 of visible bids is the one that should be traded and a market with $178.67 is the one that should be inferred from — and the tab has had it backwards.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-card">
            <h3 class="panel-title"><span class="pm-badge pm-fair">3 · THE HIKE COMPLEX IS COHERENT TO WITHIN THE SPREAD</span> 43.5 + 32.5 against 58.5 forces P(hikes at both meetings) ≥ 17.5 — a ≥40.2% conditional, and history says that is conservative</h3>
            <div class="pm-idea-date">Noted Aug 10, 2026 <span class="pm-reviewed">· Reviewed Aug 27, 2026</span><a class="pm-card-link" href="https://polymarket.com/event/fed-rate-hike-in-2026" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open this market on Polymarket">View on Polymarket ↗</a></div>
            <p>Three walked books, one arithmetic constraint. Sept-hike prints <strong>43.5</strong>, the Dec 8–9 leg <strong>32.5</strong>, any-hike-in-2026 <strong>58.5</strong>. Since a hike at either meeting implies a hike in the year, P(A∪B) ≤ 58.5, so the books require <strong>P(hikes at both) ≥ 17.5 points — a conditional of at least 40.2%</strong> given a September move, and that is before the Oct 27–28 meeting is counted at all, which only pushes the implied joint higher. Is 40.2% plausible? In every modern tightening cycle a first hike was followed by another inside two meetings far more often than not. So the complex is not just coherent, it is coherent with room to spare — no arbitrage, no dislocation, nothing to fade. That is the finding: <strong>a deep book that survives its own inequality is evidence the noise on this tab is coming from somewhere else</strong>, and card 6 names where.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-card">
            <h3 class="panel-title"><span class="pm-badge pm-rich">4 · THE BOOKS SAY 80.0% OF RECESSIONS COME WITH AN INTERMEETING CUT</span> No-cuts 85.7 → all-cut probability 14.3, of which the unscheduled leg is 6.0 · recession prints 7.5</h3>
            <div class="pm-idea-date">Noted Aug 10, 2026 <span class="pm-reviewed">· Reviewed Aug 27, 2026</span><a class="pm-card-link" href="https://polymarket.com/event/fed-emergency-rate-cut-before-2027" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open this market on Polymarket">View on Polymarket ↗</a></div>
            <p>Four walked books, read as one distribution. No-cuts at <strong>85.7 (85.6 × 261.5 / 85.8 × 4,209.7)</strong> puts all 2026 cut probability at <strong>14.3</strong>. The emergency leg at <strong>6.0 (5 × 1,288.1 / 7 × 313.6)</strong> is <strong>42.0% of that</strong> — the market says nearly half of all easing, if it comes, arrives outside a scheduled meeting. Against a recession leg at <strong>7.5</strong> that implies <strong>80.0% of recession-worlds carry an unscheduled cut</strong>. History is thinner than that: unscheduled easing clusters in 1998, 2001, 2007–08 and 2020, and several genuine recessions produced none at all. The tab’s own transmission map already wrote the modern answer — the BoE bought gilts with Bank Rate untouched, SVB got the BTFP. <strong>Facilities, not cuts.</strong> This is the cleanest priced disagreement on the tab, and it is why the emergency leg is the #3 ticket rather than a footnote.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-card">
            <h3 class="panel-title"><span class="pm-badge pm-watch">5 · THREE UNRELATED TAILS, ONE PRICE</span> Recession 7.5 · SPX touches −31% 7.5 · the 10-year touches 5.5% 7.35 — three different worlds quoted to within 0.2 of a point</h3>
            <div class="pm-idea-date">Noted Aug 10, 2026 <span class="pm-reviewed">· Reviewed Aug 27, 2026</span><a class="pm-card-link" href="https://polymarket.com/event/us-recession-by-end-of-2026" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open this market on Polymarket">View on Polymarket ↗</a></div>
            <p>An NBER-or-two-negative-quarters recession, a 1-minute candle 31% below the index, and a 78 bp back-up in the 10-year all print between <strong>7.35 and 7.5</strong> tonight. They are not the same event and they do not have the same shape: the recession leg is a slow data event resolved by BEA advance estimates, the SPX leg is an instantaneous wick, and the rate leg is a first-passage on a series with 4.3 bp/day of volatility. Their books could not be more different either — recession offers <strong>6,190.2 shares of bids at 7¢</strong>, the SPX leg <strong>12,219.3</strong>, the 10-year rung <strong>181.3 shares inside 3.4¢</strong>. When independent markets converge on a single number it usually means one of them is being priced off the others by traders who have not read the resolution text. Two of the three carry real depth, which is the only reason this row is an observation and not a spread trade.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-card">
            <h3 class="panel-title"><span class="pm-badge pm-rich">6 · THE MID IS OURS AND THE TAPE DISAGREES BY 7 POINTS</span> Rung 1 mid 34.0, last trade 27.0, our own 698.02-share lift at 25.21¢ thirty-five minutes before the snapshot</h3>
            <div class="pm-idea-date">Noted Aug 10, 2026 <span class="pm-reviewed">· Reviewed Aug 27, 2026</span><a class="pm-card-link" href="https://polymarket.com/event/how-high-will-10-year-treasury-yield-go-before-2027" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open this market on Polymarket">View on Polymarket ↗</a></div>
            <p>Restated because it now governs ten tickets rather than one card. At <strong>21:54Z</strong> the account cleared the rung-1 offer stack for 698.02 shares at a <strong>25.21¢</strong> VWAP; at <strong>22:29Z</strong> the book read <strong>31 × 43 / 37 × 100</strong> with the next ask at <strong>55</strong>. The 34.0 midpoint is the arithmetic mean of a bid nobody has hit and an offer we created by removing everything under it. The tape says <strong>27.0</strong>. The gap between those two numbers is the single most useful fact on this tab, because it is the same gap that makes the resting offers in tickets #1 and #4 sensible: an 18-point air pocket above the touch is a standing invitation to whoever sells into the next buyer who behaves the way we did tonight. The page manufactured the dislocation; the page can also be on the other side of it.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-card">
            <h3 class="panel-title"><span class="pm-badge pm-rich">7 · MARKED $3,582.74, REALISABLE $268.35 — A 92.5% HAIRCUT</span> And the ratio is worst exactly where the position is biggest</h3>
            <div class="pm-idea-date">Noted Aug 10, 2026 <span class="pm-reviewed">· Reviewed Aug 27, 2026</span><a class="pm-card-link" href="https://polymarket.com/event/how-high-will-10-year-treasury-yield-go-before-2027" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open this market on Polymarket">View on Polymarket ↗</a></div>
            <p>Leg by leg, mark against what the bid stacks actually pay: rung 1 <strong>1,046.7 sh / $178.67</strong> against 8,880.3 held, rung 2 a real stack of <strong>173.0 sh / $16.85</strong> inside 9.7¢ before a cliff to 3.5¢ against 2,868.2 held, rung 3 <strong>181.3 sh / $6.63</strong> inside 3.4¢ against 1,812.5 held. Total mark <strong>$3,582.74</strong>, total realisable <strong>$268.35</strong>, haircut <strong>92.5%</strong>. What the tab did not say last run is the corollary: <em>the mark is not wrong</em>. These legs pay $1.00 a share at resolution, so $3,582.74 is a defensible expectation and $268.35 is a liquidity fact — the position is a hold-to-Dec-31 bet whose only early exits are the resting offers in #1 and #4. Publishing both numbers side by side, forever, is the correction. Calling either one “value” is the error.</p>
            <p><strong>Friday afternoon walk (19:08Z):</strong> Four rungs now walk, since rung 0 has a book: mark <strong>$2,738.55</strong>, realisable <strong>$615.82</strong>, haircut <strong>77.5%</strong>. Leg by leg: rung 0 $211.94 → $194.71 (61.10¢), rung 1 $2,112.82 → $296.26 (3.52¢, 975 sh bidless), rung 2 $296.53 → $113.91, rung 3 $117.26 → $10.94. Rungs 1–3 alone: $2,526.61 / $421.11, 83.3%.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-card">
            <h3 class="panel-title"><span class="pm-badge pm-fair">8 · THE INFLATION BARRIER IS A TENTH HIGHER THAN THE TICKER SAYS</span> “Increased by <em>greater than</em>” plus one-decimal BLS reporting makes 4.5% resolve NO — and this is the only book on the tab with real depth on both sides</h3>
            <div class="pm-idea-date">Noted Aug 10, 2026 <span class="pm-reviewed">· Reviewed Aug 27, 2026</span><a class="pm-card-link" href="https://polymarket.com/event/how-high-will-inflation-get-in-2026" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open this market on Polymarket">View on Polymarket ↗</a></div>
            <p>Two things nobody on this tab had checked. First, the resolution text: YES requires CPI to have “increased by <strong>greater than</strong> the listed percent”, resolved off a release that “reports inflation over 12 month periods to <strong>only one decimal point</strong>” — so a 4.5% print resolves NO and the effective barrier is <strong>4.6%</strong>, a full tenth above the strike everyone quotes. Second, the book: <strong>20 × 1,712.21 / 21 × 764.24</strong>, with <strong>$959.94 of bids inside 19¢ and $652.59 of offers inside 22¢</strong>. That is the only genuinely two-sided market on this entire tab — every other row is deep on one side, thin on the other, or a canyon. Headline is 3.5%, core 2.6% and falling, five prints remain, and the <strong>Aug 12</strong> release is the first test. A one-tenth barrier correction on a 20.5¢ leg is worth more than most of the modelling this page does.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-card">
            <h3 class="panel-title"><span class="pm-badge pm-watch">9 · THE TWO ROWS THAT ARE NOT MARKETS</span> Mortgage 8 × 37.65 / 89 × 96.42 — an 81-point canyon with $4.57 of bids · stagflation, $19.16 of two-sided liquidity inside a point</h3>
            <div class="pm-idea-date">Noted Aug 10, 2026 <span class="pm-reviewed">· Reviewed Aug 27, 2026</span><a class="pm-card-link" href="https://polymarket.com/event/will-the-30-year-mortgage-rate-hit-in-2026" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open this market on Polymarket">View on Polymarket ↗</a></div>
            <p>Two rows this tab quotes with a straight face and shouldn’t. The mortgage 7.00% leg: five bid levels totalling <strong>117.6 shares and $4.57</strong>, three ask levels totalling 127.0 shares, a reported midpoint of <strong>48.5</strong> that has never been transactable at any size. The stagflation leg: <strong>225.1 shares bid ($12.72) and 105.8 offered ($6.44)</strong> inside a point of its 5.85 mid. Between them, <strong>$23.73</strong> of executable liquidity supporting two rows that have appeared in this tab’s reasoning for ten days — the mortgage row as a transmission-channel anchor, the stagflation row as corroboration against the gold book. Both are hereby demoted to context. The mortgage barrier is genuinely live (PMMS 6.66% on Jul 30, a 6.70–7.25% implied band today), which is why ticket #9 rests a bid in the hole rather than deleting the row.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-card">
            <h3 class="panel-title"><span class="pm-badge pm-watch">10 · THE SPX SKEW IS TEXTBOOK AND ITS FRONT RUNG IS A 0.93-SHARE QUOTE</span> 22.1 × 54 / 23 × 0.93, then a gap to 28.8 — the whole offer side buys 84.0 shares for $23.90</h3>
            <div class="pm-idea-date">Noted Aug 10, 2026 <span class="pm-reviewed">· Reviewed Aug 27, 2026</span><a class="pm-card-link" href="https://polymarket.com/event/spx-hit-dec-2026" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open this market on Polymarket">View on Polymarket ↗</a></div>
            <p>The down-touch ladder does two contradictory things at once. Its implied volatilities are the most professional-looking numbers on the page — <strong>24.6% / 27.9% / 33.3%</strong> across −17% / −23% / −31%, a clean monotone put skew — and its most-quoted rung is a fiction. The 6,200 leg reads <strong>22.1 × 54 / 23 × 0.93</strong>, and above that best offer of <em>0.93 shares</em> the stack jumps to 24 × 5 and then 28.8 × 78.1: buying the entire visible offer side gets you <strong>84.0 shares for $23.90</strong>. Meanwhile the 5,200 leg carries <strong>12,219.3 shares of bids at 7¢</strong>. Same event family, same expiry, same resolution source — a 476× depth difference between the near strike and the far one, with the size sitting on the tail. That inversion of the usual liquidity pattern is why ticket #5 sells the far rung and why the 6,200 mid is used only as a trigger, never as a price.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-card">
            <h3 class="panel-title"><span class="pm-badge pm-fair">11 · THE BASKET THAT LOOKED FREE AND WASN'T</span> <span class="pm-badge pm-fair">✅ HOUSE RULE #4 EARNED ITS KEEP</span> Median-home-value legs sum to 95.75¢ on gamma and 101.20¢ on the CLOB — the arb was an artifact of the wrong endpoint</h3>
            <div class="pm-idea-date">Noted Aug 10, 2026 <span class="pm-reviewed">· Reviewed Aug 27, 2026</span><a class="pm-card-link" href="https://polymarket.com/event/what-will-the-median-home-value-in-the-us-be-on-september-30-20260630175540363" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open this market on Polymarket">View on Polymarket ↗</a></div>
            <p>The seven buckets of the Sep 30 median-home-value market are mutually exclusive and exhaustive, so the YES legs must sum to $1. Gamma's prices sum to <strong>95.75¢</strong> — a 4.25-point free lunch, and exactly the kind of thing that gets published by a page that quotes discovery data as though it were executable. Walking all seven CLOB books instead: the best asks sum to <strong>101.20¢</strong>, so buying the complete basket <em>costs</em> 1.2 points over par, and the best bids sum to <strong>90.30¢</strong>, so selling it gives up 9.7. There is no arb; there is a 10.9-point round-trip spread, which is what a $10.7k-liquidity event with three dead tails actually looks like.</p>
            <div class="pm-exec"><strong>THE TICKET:</strong> <strong>No trade — this card exists as evidence.</strong> House rule #4 (gamma is discovery only, never a quoted price) has now caught a false arbitrage as well as the mortgage row's phantom 48.5 midpoint. Any future basket claim on this page must show the summed <em>book</em> sides, not the summed mids.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="pm-footnote" data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read the full footnote">Ten dislocations, and the section’s scope changed tonight: it now covers the whole rates complex rather than the 10-year ladder, because the ladder is <strong>8.4% of the complex by tradeable depth and was 100% of the tab’s attention</strong>. New tonight: the inverted gold smile (row 1 — the only ladder of three whose implied vol falls with strike, and the account owns its rich end), the depth ratio behind the kill-switch (row 2 — <strong>857×</strong>), the hike complex’s coherence bound (row 3 — the books force P(both meetings) ≥ 17.5, and no arb survives it), the emergency-cut decomposition (row 4 — <strong>42.0% of all cut probability is unscheduled</strong>, implying 80.0% of recession-worlds carry an intermeeting cut), the three-tails-one-price convergence (row 5), and the CPI barrier correction (row 8 — “greater than” plus one-decimal reporting moves the strike to <strong>4.6%</strong>). Carried forward and re-verified: the mid we made ourselves (row 6), and the $3,582.74 / $268.35 split, now stated with its corollary — the mark is a defensible expectation, it is simply not liquidity (row 7). Demoted to context: the mortgage and stagflation rows, <strong>$23.73 of executable liquidity between them</strong> (row 9). Every quote walked at 22:29 UTC Aug 10, Monday post-close; implied volatilities inverted in-run from the driftless touch formula over the 143 days to Dec 31, using the barrier distances this tab already publishes. <strong>Eleven dislocations tracked</strong> (target 8–12) — #11 added tonight when a new complex, the Sep 30 median-US-home-value ladder, produced a 4.25-point basket "arb" on gamma prices that vanished the moment its seven CLOB books were actually walked.</p>
      </section>

