      <section class="table-section" aria-label="Path to six percent and catalysts">
        <div class="table-header-row">
          <h2 class="section-title">The Path to 5.25% — and the Calendar That Decides 6%</h2>
          <span class="price-note">30Y monthly marks are Treasury.gov par-yield monthly averages, computed in-run from the daily series (pipeline restored Aug 10) · spikes are daily closes · catalysts from official calendars</span>
        </div>
        <div class="table-wrapper">
          <table class="pm-table">
              <thead><tr><th>2026</th><th class="col-num">Jan</th><th class="col-num">Feb</th><th class="col-num">Mar</th><th class="col-num">Apr</th><th class="col-num">May</th><th class="col-num">Jun</th><th class="col-num">Jul</th><th class="col-num">Aug</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="pm-co">30Y average</td><td class="col-num">4.84%</td><td class="col-num">4.76%</td><td class="col-num">4.85%</td><td class="col-num">4.91%</td><td class="col-num">5.03%</td><td class="col-num">4.95%</td><td class="col-num">5.10%</td><td class="col-num">5.21%*</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="pm-co">What happened</td><td colspan="8"><span class="lf-cell" data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read the rest">Feb basing → May 19: <strong>5.198%</strong>, first take-out of the 2023 high ("highest since before the GFC") → June cool-off to a 4.95% average → <strong>Jul 31: 5.27%, the 2026 high as of Aug 10 (since taken out by 5.31 on Aug 17)</strong> and the print this tab missed while its yield pipeline was dark → an August round trip: 5.23 (3rd) → <strong>5.17 (5th, the month’s low)</strong> → 5.22 (6th) → 5.19 (7th, jobs-print Friday) → <strong>5.25 (10th)</strong>. Six August sessions, an 8 bp range, and no direction. *August average is month-to-date over those six prints.</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td class="pm-co">The supply side</td><td colspan="8"><span class="lf-cell" data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read the rest">FY26 deficit $1.37T through nine months, CRFB projecting <strong>≥$2T</strong> · May 13 30Y auction bid-to-cover <strong>2.30 vs 2.40</strong> ten-auction average · March TIC: <strong>$138.4B net foreign selling</strong> (Japan −$47.7B, China −$41B) · ACM 10Y term premium +0.83%, a decade high but only mid-range vs pre-2008 — the room above is the thesis. The room below: both 2026 spikes died within 5 bp of 5.20, and June's retreat to 4.82% is what real-money demand at these yields looks like.</span></td></tr>
