      <!-- ===================== WHAT 6% BREAKS ===================== -->
      <section class="table-section" aria-label="Economic model of a six percent long bond">
        <div class="table-header-row">
          <h2 class="section-title">What a 6% Long Bond Does — The Transmission Map</h2>
          <span class="price-note">Channel-by-channel model of the economy and the market at 30Y ≥ 6.00% · figures derived from sourced inputs listed in Methodology</span>
        </div>
        <div class="table-wrapper">
          <table class="pm-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Channel</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Where it lands</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="pm-co">Mortgages &amp; housing</td><td class="trs-prose">PMMS 30Y mortgage prices off the long end at a 145–200 bp spread. 6.00% Treasury ⇒ <strong>7.4–8.0% mortgage</strong>. On a $336k loan (median-price, 20% down) the P&amp;I payment goes $2,159 → ≈$2,440: <strong>+13%</strong>, on top of the cumulative repricing since 2021.</td><td class="trs-prose">Affordability through the 2023 lows toward early-1980s territory; existing-home lock-in deepens (nobody trades a 3% mortgage for 8%); transactions and starts stall; homebuilders and the real-estate services complex (the fund is short Z, EXPI, CBRE, CWK) take the direct hit.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="pm-co">The federal interest bill</td><td class="trs-prose">≈$41T of debt (the Polymarket peak-debt book priced $41T at 54¢ in the Jul 31 snapshot) rolling into a 6% long end while the deficit runs ≥$2T. Each +100 bp across the curve compounds toward <strong>+$300–400B/yr of interest expense</strong> as the stock rolls over.</td><td class="trs-prose"><span class="lf-cell" data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read the rest">Interest expense — already past defense spending — becomes the deficit's fastest-growing line: more issuance ⇒ higher yields ⇒ more interest ⇒ more issuance. This loop is <em>why</em> a 6% touch is plausible without an inflation spiral: it's a term-premium revolt, the bond-vigilante mechanism Yardeni says already had its first Warsh test on Jul 30 (his note ran Jul 29).</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td class="pm-co">Corporate credit &amp; AI capex</td><td class="trs-prose">$1T+ of 2026 corporate issuance — much of it funding the AI datacenter buildout — reprices: IG lands ~7–7.5%, HY 9–11% once spreads widen (2022: IG +120 bp, HY +330 bp). Every hyperscaler lease and GPU depreciation schedule was underwritten at a 4-handle risk-free.</td><td class="trs-prose">The AI capex machine slows at the margin; long-duration equity gets the double hit (discount rate + capex doubt). The "AI bubble burst" book (19.7¢, Jul 31) and the semis-heavy model books on this site are the read-through. Refi wall 2027 turns from footnote to headline.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="pm-co">Banks &amp; levered holders</td><td class="trs-prose"><span class="lf-cell" data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read the rest">A 30Y bought at 5.2% loses ≈<strong>11%</strong> at 6.0% (modified duration ≈15; convexity trims the realized loss) — that's the worst case, on the newest paper; seasoned 20-year holdings lose ≈9%. System AFS/HTM unrealized losses blow back through the 2023 peak (~$690B). The ~$1T hedge-fund Treasury basis trade is 2026's LDI: a VaR shock forces selling into a falling market.</span></td><td class="trs-prose"><span class="lf-cell" data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read the rest">This is the disorderly path: SVB-2023 and UK-gilts-2022 both started here. Note what history actually says about the response: <em>facilities and asset purchases, not emergency cuts</em> — the BoE bought gilts with Bank Rate untouched; SVB got the BTFP. Watch the bank-failure books and repo/SRF usage — the accident, if it comes, shows up there first.</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td class="pm-co">The stock market</td><td class="trs-prose"><span class="lf-cell" data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read the rest">SPX ≈7,490 at ~24–25x forward = <strong>4.1% earnings yield vs a 5.4–5.6% 10-year</strong> in the scenario — an equity risk premium of ≈ −1.4%, unprecedented outside 1999–2000. Partial normalization (ERP to −0.4%…+0.2%) ⇒ 17.5–20x ⇒ <strong>SPX 5,300–6,100, a −19% to −29% drawdown</strong>; full normalization to +1% ERP ⇒ ~15x ⇒ under 4,800. History: 2022's hiking cycle took SPX −19% from a 21x start; Oct 2023's 5% 10-year took −10% from 19x; today's ~25x start is the amplifier. 1994's +250 bp left SPX flat — but from 15x.</span></td><td class="trs-prose"><span class="lf-cell" data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read the rest">Long-duration megacap AI leads the downside (it led the upside for the same reason); relative winners: insurers reinvesting at 6%, short-duration value, energy. The SPX 6,200/5,800 touch legs sit at scenario-fair only under this page's calibration — their prices bundle every crash cause at once and carry little clean rates signal on their own.</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td class="pm-co">The Fed's bind</td><td class="trs-prose"><span class="lf-cell" data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read the rest">Warsh inherits 3.5% CPI (energy +15.7% y/y, war premium), a hiking bias (three dissents), and a long end that sells off <em>anyway</em>. Orderly path: he hikes (Sep 57.5¢), credibility slowly caps the long end — 1995, not 1994. Fiscal-dominance path: hikes don't help because the selloff is about <em>supply</em>, not expected inflation — late 2025 already showed cuts with a rising 30Y.</span></td><td class="trs-prose"><span class="lf-cell" data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read the rest">The perverse trades: rate <em>hikes</em> that the long end ignores, then an accident answered with <em>facilities</em> (the UK 2022 template: gilts 3.6→5.1% in 3 sessions, up to £65B of purchases announced — ~£19B used — Bank Rate untouched, a government gone in 49 days). The Sep-hike book and the emergency-cut book can both matter in the same year — but the cut book is the rich way to own that story; the facilities era is exactly why.</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td class="pm-co">The other side</td><td class="trs-prose">Base case (model ≈82%): the pattern of 2026 holds — both spikes died within 5 bp of 5.20, June retraced 40 bp to 4.82%, core CPI is 2.6% and falling, each hike the market prices <em>caps</em> the long end a little more, and 5%+ all-in yields keep pulling insurers and pensions off the sidelines.</td><td class="trs-prose"><span class="lf-cell" data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read the rest">If that's the world: the "how low" ladder (10Y below 3.9% at 8.8¢, Jul 31) and the SPX up-touches are the trades, and this page's basket burns to zero by New Year's. The BofA survey (62% expect a 6-handle within 12 months) cuts both ways — it's supportive, and it means the thesis is already consensus among the people who set the deep books' prices.</span></td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ===================== METHODOLOGY ===================== -->
<section class="table-section" aria-label="Rates resolution sources and risk">
        <div class="table-header-row">
          <h2 class="section-title">Resolution — Who Decides These Markets, and Where It Can Go Wrong</h2>
          <span class="pm-sec-date">Ideas as of Aug 10, 2026 · re-checked Aug 27, 6:49 AM PT (Wednesday post-print morning walk)</span>
          <span class="price-note" data-longform="2" data-longform-label="Read the full note">Four different definitions of “touch” across one tab — a daily par yield, an upper bound, a 1-minute candle, a futures settlement and a weekly survey · two of tonight’s tickets exist <em>because</em> of the resolution text, not despite it · read before sizing anything</span>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-cards">
          <div class="panel-card">
            <h3 class="panel-title"><span class="pm-badge pm-fair">THE LADDER — A DAILY PRINT, AND A WINDOW THAT OPENED IN 2025</span> The rule verbatim, and the 152 sessions that have already run against it</h3>
            <div class="pm-idea-date">Idea added Aug 10, 2026 <span class="pm-reviewed">· Reviewed Aug 27, 2026</span><a class="pm-card-link" href="https://polymarket.com/event/how-high-will-10-year-treasury-yield-go-before-2027" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open this market on Polymarket">View on Polymarket ↗</a></div>
            <p>“This market will resolve to ‘Yes’ if the Treasury 10-year yield reaches or is higher than the listed value <strong>for any date between [Nov 11, 2025 and Dec 31, 2026]</strong>. … The resolution source for this market is the Department of the treasury, specially the data listed under ‘<strong>Daily Treasury Par Yield Curve Rates</strong>’ for the column ‘<strong>10 Yr</strong>’.” Three consequences the tab has never spelled out. First, the barrier is a <strong>published daily par yield</strong>, not an intraday tick — a session that trades through 5.00% and prints 4.97% pays nothing, which is precisely the discrete-monitoring shift the model already charges about 2 points for. Second, the window is longer than the calendar year: it has been running since <strong>Nov 11, 2025</strong>. Third, the record so far is checkable and now checked — of the <strong>152 sessions of 2026 in that file, the highest 10 Yr print is 4.75% on Jul 31</strong>, today’s 4.72% is the second-highest, and <strong>zero sessions have reached 4.80%</strong>. Rungs 0 through 5 are all still live; none of them has been quietly resolved by a print the tab missed while its yield pipeline was dark.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-card">
            <h3 class="panel-title"><span class="pm-badge pm-watch">THE FED BOOKS — AN UPPER BOUND, A ROUNDING RULE, AND “CREDIBLE REPORTING” AGAIN</span> 12.5 bp rounds UP to 25 · emergency cuts count toward the cut total · the year book cannot resolve NO until after Dec 8–9</h3>
            <div class="pm-idea-date">Idea added Aug 10, 2026 <span class="pm-reviewed">· Reviewed Aug 27, 2026</span><a class="pm-card-link" href="https://polymarket.com/event/fed-decision-in-september-762" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open this market on Polymarket">View on Polymarket ↗</a></div>
            <p>Every Fed row on this tab resolves on “<strong>the upper bound of the target federal funds range</strong>”, never the effective rate and never a Fed-speak signal. The meeting books resolve to the change “versus the level it was prior to” that meeting, and carry a rounding rule worth knowing: “if the target federal funds rate is changed to a level not expressed in the displayed options, the change will be <strong>rounded up to the nearest 25</strong>” — an unlikely 12.5 bp move resolves the 25-bp bracket, not the no-change one. The year book resolves YES if the upper bound rises “at any point between January 1, 2026 and the Fed’s [final] 2026 meeting”, primary source federalreserve.gov, “<strong>however a consensus of credible reporting may also be used</strong>” — the same soft clause that governs the largest-company books, here attached to something far less ambiguous. And the cut-count book explicitly counts <strong>emergency cuts outside scheduled meetings</strong> toward the total and stays open to Dec 31, 11:59 PM ET to catch them, which is exactly why the no-cuts leg and the emergency leg can be read as one distribution in ticket #3.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-card">
            <h3 class="panel-title"><span class="pm-badge pm-watch">THREE MARKETS, THREE INCOMPATIBLE DEFINITIONS OF “TOUCH”</span> SPX = a 1-minute candle high on Yahoo · gold = a CME <em>Active Month</em> settlement · mortgage = a weekly Freddie Mac survey</h3>
            <div class="pm-idea-date">Idea added Aug 10, 2026 <span class="pm-reviewed">· Reviewed Aug 27, 2026</span><a class="pm-card-link" href="https://polymarket.com/event/spx-hit-dec-2026" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open this market on Polymarket">View on Polymarket ↗</a></div>
            <p>The tab’s three touch ladders are quoted in the same column and monitored on three different clocks, which is the hidden reason their implied volatilities are not comparable. SPX resolves off “any <strong>1-minute candle</strong>” high or low “as reflected in Yahoo Finance’s 1-minute interval data”, regular trading hours only — effectively continuous monitoring, and an instantaneous wick counts. Gold resolves off “the official CME <strong>settlement</strong> price for the <strong>Active Month</strong>” contract, where the Active Month is the nearest delivery-cycle month (Feb/Apr/Jun/Aug/Oct/Dec) that is <em>not</em> the spot month, and it rolls automatically on the First Position Date — so an intraday spike to $5,000 that settles at $4,960 pays nothing, and the contract being measured changes underneath the holder several times before Dec 31. The mortgage row resolves off the <strong>weekly Freddie Mac PMMS</strong> — roughly 20 observations left in the year, the coarsest monitoring on the page, which makes a barrier the spread band already straddles genuinely uncertain. Same word, three instruments, and only one of them is near-continuous.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-card">
            <h3 class="panel-title"><span class="pm-badge pm-rich">WHERE THE TAIL TICKETS CAN GO WRONG — REVISIONS, ADVANCE ESTIMATES, AND A SETTLEMENT THAT LANDS IN 2027</span> The recession leg may not resolve until the Q4 advance estimate, weeks after every other row on this tab has paid</h3>
            <div class="pm-idea-date">Idea added Aug 10, 2026 <span class="pm-reviewed">· Reviewed Aug 27, 2026</span><a class="pm-card-link" href="https://polymarket.com/event/us-recession-by-end-of-2026" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open this market on Polymarket">View on Polymarket ↗</a></div>
            <p>Four ways the short-tail basket in ticket #10 can be right about the world and still lose. <strong>One:</strong> the recession leg resolves on <strong>advance estimates</strong> and reaches back to <strong>Q2 2025</strong> — a revision that turns an old quarter negative can trigger it, and it “will stay open until the Advance estimate of Q4 2026 is published”, which lands well after Dec 31. Capital is committed past the horizon the rest of the tab assumes. <strong>Two:</strong> the inflation leg freezes on the year-end release — “any revisions to previously released CPI figures will not be counted” after it — so a hot print that is later revised away still resolves YES, and the ticket is short exactly that asymmetry. <strong>Three:</strong> the year-end state-of-the-economy book resolves on the year-end U-3 and CPI-U prints and defaults if they are not published by <strong>Jan 31, 2027</strong>, so a data-release disruption is a live settlement risk on a $19.16 book. <strong>Four:</strong> the ladder’s own source is a government web page that this site has already watched go stale for <strong>eleven days</strong>. Every one of these is a reason the tail carries are capped at $750 combined rather than sized to their apparent edge — and prediction markets can resolve against you on oracle judgment alone.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="pm-footnote" data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read the full footnote">Four cards, all rewritten tonight against the live market descriptions rather than from memory, and two of them changed a ticket. The ladder card produced the verified fact that <strong>zero of 152 sessions in the 2026 par-yield file have printed a 10 Yr at or above 4.80%</strong> — the year’s high is 4.75% on Jul 31, today is 4.72% — which is what makes the rung-0 watch (#11) a liquidity problem rather than a modelling one. The inflation card produced the <strong>“greater than” plus one-decimal reading that lifts the effective barrier to 4.6%</strong>, which is the reason ticket #6 exists at all. Unchanged and worth repeating: these are not exchange-cleared instruments; resolution runs through published data and, on the Fed rows, through a “consensus of credible reporting” clause that has already misfired elsewhere on this site. The 10-year rungs monitor on daily closes, gold on CME settlements of a contract that rolls, SPX on 1-minute candles, mortgages on a weekly survey — four monitoring frequencies, one column of prices, and a comparison between them is only ever approximate. Fictitious research page; nothing here is financial advice.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="table-section" aria-label="Treasuries methodology">
        <div class="table-header-row">
          <h2 class="section-title">Methodology, Sources &amp; Honesty Box</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-method">
          <div class="pm-method-block"><span class="pm-method-h">The touch model</span>
            <p data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read this section">P(30Y prints ≥6.00% on a daily close by Dec 31) uses the exact drifted first-passage probability — Φ((μT−a)/σ√T) + e^(2μa/σ²)·Φ((−a−μT)/σ√T) — with a Broadie–Glasserman–Kou barrier shift (+0.5826·σ√Δt) because the ladder resolves on <em>one daily print</em>, not the intraday path. That distinction is not pedantry: in Oct 2023 the 10-year famously “hit 5%” intraday (5.02%) while the daily par yield peaked at <strong>4.98%</strong> — the most celebrated touch of the last cycle would have resolved NO on these markets’ terms. <strong>Re-derived Aug 10 on a live curve for the first time since Jul 30:</strong> a = <strong>75 bp</strong> from the 5.25% close, T = <strong>101</strong> trading days, σ = <strong>4.0 bp/day</strong> (measured: 20d 4.20, 40d 3.80, 60d 3.89, full-2026 3.76). <strong>With zero drift, P = 5.4%.</strong> With 2026’s realised +0.258 bp/day carried forward, P = 16.2% — but <strong>67% of that number is the drift</strong>, and the drift has a standard error of 0.306, so <strong>t = 0.84, p = 0.40</strong>; its 95% interval alone maps to P = 0.8%–68.2%. This tab now leads with the martingale number and shows the trend case beside it. <strong>Two prior errors, disclosed:</strong> the old central case of 18% never reproduced from its own stated inputs (a = 79, T = 105, σ = 5.5, +15 bp gives <strong>20.8%</strong>), and the σ = 5.5 it used was attributed to a July vol spike that the data does not contain — <strong>July’s 30Y daily-change sd was 3.58 bp</strong>, the fourth-lowest month of 2026.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="pm-method-block"><span class="pm-method-h">Conditionals — and why they are disclosed</span>
            <p data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read this section">The per-row P|6% / P|no-6% decomposition is <strong>retired</strong>. Its conditionals were hand-set, its P6 was mostly drift, and the “cal.” rows had P|no-6% calibrated so that fair ≡ mid — carrying, by construction, no information at all. The ladder is now priced by two models fitted to the 2026 10-year directly, and every non-ladder row is marked <strong>NO MODEL</strong> rather than given a fair this page cannot defend. One published conditional is explicitly withdrawn (see New #11): the claim that long-end selloffs widen 30s10s, which justified rung 3’s 30% conditional, fails on 2026 — the ten largest 30Y selloff days widened the spread five times, mean +0.7 bp, and β(Δ10Y on Δ30Y) = <strong>1.056</strong> implies bear-<em>flattening</em>.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="pm-method-block"><span class="pm-method-h">Rows marked “cal.”</span>
            <p data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read this section">Gone, and so is the label. Where a row has no yield model behind it — the Fed complex, the SPX down-touch ladder, gold, stagflation, CPI, recession, the mortgage row — the table now prints <strong>NO MODEL</strong> and leaves the model columns blank. That is a demotion of about half the table, and it is honest: those books move on things this page does not model, and calibrating a fair to their own mid was a way of appearing to price them without doing so.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="pm-method-block"><span class="pm-method-h">The two models, and the mixture</span>
            <p data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read this section"><strong>Martingale:</strong> zero-drift random walk on the 10-year, σ = 4.4 bp/day (measured 4.26–4.60 across windows), same first-passage machinery and BGK shift. <strong>AR(1):</strong> fitted on 2026, φ = 0.98089, attractor 4.549%, half-life 35.9 trading days. <strong>The fair is their equal-weight mixture</strong>, and the honest reason is that neither survives on its own. The AR(1) is probably Dickey–Fuller small-sample bias with a story attached: simulating true random walks at n = 151 produces a φ̂ at or below 0.98089 <strong>52.8% of the time</strong>, and an ADF test has only ~10% power against it here — so “the series mean-reverts” is not something this data can establish. The martingale, meanwhile, ignores that yields have looked mean-reverting to everyone pricing these books all year. The mixture lands within a point of the market on both live rungs (35.0 vs 34.00, 13.8 vs 14.15), which is what an efficient market facing genuine model uncertainty should look like. <strong>We are not claiming the mixture is right. We are claiming the market is not obviously wrong, which is what this tab spent ten days asserting.</strong></p>
          </div>
          <div class="pm-method-block"><span class="pm-method-h">The smile, and what a constant σ does to it</span>
            <p data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read this section">Inverting each rung to the σ that makes it fair at zero drift gives <strong>1.94 / 3.11 / 3.38 / 4.48 / 4.97 / 5.87 bp per day</strong> across the 4.8 → 6.00 strikes — a clean monotone smile. Against a rising smile, <strong>any</strong> constant-σ model must call the near rungs cheap and the far rungs rich. This tab reported exactly that pattern for ten days and read it as a finding; it was the assumption looking at itself. The implied-σ column is published so the smile is visible rather than laundered through a fair.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="pm-method-block"><span class="pm-method-h">Books, and how thin they are</span>
            <p data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read this section">Every executable quote was CLOB-verified (bid/ask/depth, asset_id matched, timestamp checked) at <strong>22:29 UTC Aug 10</strong>; gamma-only prices are never shown as executable, which is why the mortgage row wears NO BOOK. <strong>The depth numbers matter more than the prices.</strong> The 10-year ladder marks $3,582.74 and liquidates into its own bid stacks for <strong>$268.35</strong>: the 5.0% leg fills 1,047 of 8,880 shares at a 17.1¢ VWAP and 88% finds no bid at any price, while the 5.2% and 5.5% legs do fill in full — into 25,015 and 16,549 shares of penny bids. A further caution this tab did not previously make: <strong>the account’s own orders move these books.</strong> Tonight’s 698-share lift at 25.21¢ cleared the offer stack 35 minutes before the snapshot and left an 18-point air pocket above the quote, so the 34.0 mid is partly our own footprint; the tape’s last trade is 27.0.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="pm-method-block"><span class="pm-method-h">Sources</span>
            <p data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read this section">Federal Reserve <strong>H.15</strong> and <strong>Treasury.gov daily par yields</strong> — both added to this run’s data pipeline on Aug 10 and validated by reproducing the Jul 30 close of 5.21% exactly · Freddie Mac PMMS Jul 30 (6.66%) · FRED GS30 (monthly path; last 6% = May 2000) · BLS CPI Jun (3.5% / 2.6% core) · BEA PCE Jun (3.7%) · CBO/CRFB + Treasury MTS (deficit) · TreasuryDirect (auction stats) · NY Fed ACM (term premium) · Treasury TIC (foreign flows) · CME FedWatch · Polymarket CLOB (all book/mid quotes) and Data API (positions, activity) · equity closes from Finnhub, cross-checked against stockanalysis.com.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="pm-method-block"><span class="pm-method-h">Scoring the account against this tab</span>
            <p data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read this section">Seven Treasury legs were bought off these cards, and tonight’s revision makes most of them look luckier rather than smarter. Marked against the fairs published at the time: <strong>rung 1 (5.0%) filled at a 21.56¢ blended basis against a 41.7% fair then, and a 35.0% mixture now</strong> — 20 points inside the old fair, 13 inside the new one, marking <strong>+$1,015.52</strong> and realisable for $178.67. <strong>Rung 2 (5.2%) at 7.53¢ against 17.5% then, 13.8% now</strong>, +$189.57 marked. <strong>Rung 3 (5.5%) at 5.51¢ against 5.8% then, 3.4% now</strong> — at model then, above it now, +$32.31. <strong>Rung 0 (4.8%) at a 70.5¢ basis against 65.4% then, 77.6% now</strong> — the only leg the revision helps, −$11.63. The two low-ladder NO legs (3.6% and 3.7%) are short the same tail the ladder is long. <strong>This is one correlated $3.6k bet, not seven independent ones</strong>, and the honest summary of the ten days is that the page called a direction correctly and its stated reason for the call does not survive its own re-derivation.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="pm-method-block"><span class="pm-method-h">What would falsify this tab</span>
