      <section class="table-section" aria-label="Bitcoin methodology">
        <div class="table-header-row">
          <h2 class="section-title">Methodology, Sources &amp; Honesty Box</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="pm-method">
          <div class="pm-method-block"><span class="pm-method-h">The touch model</span>
            <p data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read this section">Driftless lognormal, reflection principle: <strong>P(touch B before T) = 2·N(−|ln(B/S)| / (σ√T))</strong>, S = $78,536.24 (Coinbase BTC-USD, 22:18 UTC Aug 21), σ in %/day, T in days. Symmetric by construction — the same formula prices a reach and a dip — and driftless by choice, because no drift estimate on Bitcoin has a t-statistic worth publishing. The model is the same first-passage machinery the Treasuries tab uses without the barrier shift: these markets resolve on 1-minute candles, so the continuous-monitoring assumption is close to exact.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="pm-method-block"><span class="pm-method-h">T, and what the clock does</span>
            <p data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read this section">T_aug = <strong>10.24 days</strong>, to Aug 31 23:59 ET = Sep 1 04:00Z; T_dec = <strong>132.3 days</strong>, to Dec 31 23:59 ET = Jan 1 05:00Z; both measured from the 22:18Z snapshot. Fairs are baked at those T and do not decay on the page. As the clock runs the true touch probability falls while the baked fair stays put, so the baked fair is <strong>too high</strong>: a live mid that tracks the shrinking T looks ever cheaper against it. Stale T flatters every live CHEAP badge and under-reports every RICH one — the August rows worst, since ten days is a third of their window by Aug 25. Re-bake before trusting any August verdict after Aug 25.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="pm-method-block"><span class="pm-method-h">σ, and its sensitivity</span>
            <p data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read this section">House σ = <strong>2.35%/day</strong>, the 349-day close-to-close realised (45% annualised). Alternatives measured: 10d 2.97%, 20d 2.39%, 30d 2.26%, 60d 2.00%, 90d 2.12%; Parkinson 20d 2.12%, 60d 2.04%. The σ 2.0 and σ 3.0 columns in the data bracket every fair in the table; the row notes print the <strong>implied σ</strong> — the daily vol at which the model reproduces the mid — so the reader can see which σ the market is using rather than argue about which one the page should. Every verdict is a statement about 2.35 vs that number.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="pm-method-block"><span class="pm-method-h">Binance vs Coinbase</span>
            <p data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read this section">All 51 markets resolve on the <strong>Binance BTC/USDT 1-minute candle</strong> high (reach) or low (dip). Spot, candles and realised vol on this page are <strong>Coinbase BTC-USD</strong>. Binance USDT prints typically run a few tens of dollars above Coinbase USD; the page discloses and does not adjust. It matters for exactly one row tonight — Aug $80k reach, where Coinbase&rsquo;s 79,500 high is $500 under the rung — and it is why that card says check the market page before the book.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="pm-method-block"><span class="pm-method-h">The 1–2¢ tail floor</span>
            <p data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read this section">Rows with mid &lt; 2.0¢ and fair &lt; 0.5 are labelled <strong>TAIL</strong> and publish no edge. The implied σ of 3–9%/day on those rows (Aug $40k dip 6.06%, Dec $5k dip 9.53%, Dec $1M reach 8.27%) is the price of warehousing an unsellable contract, not a vol forecast. Counting those as RICH would add seventeen badges (ten August, seven December) to the table that no one can act on. The Dec $25k dip (2.5¢, +2.5 exactly) and the Dec $170k–$200k reach legs (2.05–2.35¢) sit just above the threshold and wear FAIR; they are floor pricing too.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="pm-method-block"><span class="pm-method-h">Books, and capturable edge</span>
            <p data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read this section">Every quote is a CLOB /book snapshot with asset_id matched to the market&rsquo;s YES token, August at 22:17–22:18Z and December at 22:08–22:09Z. Can buy = the full ask side in dollars, Can sell = the full bid side; Market? is DEEP / OK / THIN / NO MARKET on those. Capturable edge for a CHEAP row = Σ(fair − askᵢ) × sizeᵢ over every ask level priced under the σ 2.35 fair, from the raw book — what a buyer walking the offer up to fair would pocket at fair: $90k $2,250 (33,150 sh), $100k $1,234 (25,747), $85k $1,162 (15,066), $95k $508 (10,231), $80k $224 (12,771), $110k $183 (12,121), Aug $75k dip $18 (2,721), Aug $72.5k dip $0 (58). Walk-outs for held legs are from the same books: all nine fill in full.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="pm-method-block"><span class="pm-method-h">What the model cannot do</span>
            <p data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read this section">It has <strong>no drift</strong>: a +25% five-session move is, to the model, a draw from a zero-mean distribution, and the next five are too. It has <strong>no regime</strong>: the SEC Aug 18 / White House Aug 19 / CFTC Aug 20 sequence is a level shift in the rule set, and the model treats Aug 21 like any other day at 2.35%. It has <strong>no skew</strong> of its own, which is precisely why it can see the market&rsquo;s: up 1.5–2.3, down 2.5–4.4. <strong>The skew is a claim</strong> — the market&rsquo;s claim that the rip mean-reverts — and this page reports it rather than trading against it. Sixteen CHEAP/RICH badges on the December ladder are one disagreement about σ, repeated.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="pm-method-block"><span class="pm-method-h">Sources</span>
            <p data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read this section">Coinbase BTC-USD spot, 24h stats and daily candles (Aug 14–21) · Polymarket CLOB /book and /midpoint for all 51 legs; data-api /positions and /value for the account · Yahoo Finance Aug 21 (open $73,013, +15.2% w/w, −36.1% y/y, ATH $126,198.07 Oct 6, 2025) · Motley Fool Aug 21 (+6.34% to $77,205, 24h $72,500–$79,320, COIN +7.53%, XRP +11.87%) · White House meeting Aug 19, SEC proposal Aug 18, CFTC statement Aug 20, Treasury buyback announcement Aug 19 as reported. Live refresh adds 51 CLOB midpoint calls per 60 s to the page&rsquo;s existing 36. Fictitious research page — not financial advice.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="pm-method-block"><span class="pm-method-h">What would falsify this tab</span>
            <p data-longform="3" data-longform-label="Read this section">A Dec up-rung mid rising to its σ 2.35 fair with spot unchanged — the market adopting the house σ — would make the skew disappear and the CHEAP badges with it; that is the test of the claim, and it is the market&rsquo;s to pass. Beyond that, tonight&rsquo;s specific commitments: (1) no BTC adds at any rung while the complex is over a third of the book; (2) re-walk all nine bid sides every run and print the haircut; (3) the Aug $80k resolution status checked before any August ticket; (4) re-bake the August fairs before Aug 25 or stop quoting them.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
      </div>

      <div class="pm-view" id="view-tracking" hidden>
      <div class="fund-header">
        <h2 class="fund-title">Decision Tracking — Scoring Every Executed Call</h2>
        <p class="fund-subtitle" data-longform="5" data-longform-label="Read the full brief">When a recommendation from this site gets actioned (add-on, new position, or sell), it moves here as a permanent ledger entry. Each row is re-scored live against the current market so the effectiveness of every decision is measurable — sells score the money saved by exiting, adds score P&amp;L since the fill. The twice-daily run appends new executions with actual average fills from the trade history.</p>
